import json
ALL=[1,10,8453,130,7777777,480,57073,1868,42220,196,42161,4663,99901,137,56,43114,143,4326,4217,5042,999]
W='research/network-risk/cycles/008/core-code-invariants-bits.work/'
RPC21='rpc eth_call on the 21 rpc URLs of research/network-risk/state/networks.json on 2026-11-23 at blocks: Ethereum 26480426, Optimism 159934675, Base 61388762, Unichain 64719767, Zora 54387144, Worldchain 38066244, Ink 61969716, Soneium 31166687, Celo 83567375, X Layer 76699099, Arbitrum One 526922205, Robinhood Chain 121713450, Elysium 10053396, Polygon 97841780, BNB Smart Chain 135340362, Avalanche 101198930, Monad 124821811, MegaETH 32671135, Tempo 50487081, Arc 32795973, HyperEVM 52064656'
cleared=[]
def C(check,result,source,chains=ALL,lens='L1'):
    cleared.append({'chainIds':chains,'lens':lens,'check':check,'result':result,'source':source})

C("Calldata width as the deployed contracts enforce it. The pool id is the hash of the PoolKey struct as it sits in memory (src/types/PoolId.sol:11-15), and take, sync, mint and the claim getters use address and uint24 arguments as mapping indexes and call targets. No line of src rejects a word with bits above the declared width, so the question is whether such a word can reach the hash, a delta slot or a mapping and give one pool or one currency a second identity.",
  "It cannot on any of the 21 deployed PoolManagers. I sent 47 eth_call requests to each of them. initialize with a clean struct returns tick 0. The same call reverts with no return data when bit 160 is set in the currency0 word, the currency1 word, the hooks word or the price word, when bit 24 is set in the fee word or the spacing word, and when the spacing word is 0xffffc4 without sign extension. The properly sign extended value -60 reaches the code and returns TickSpacingTooSmall(-60) (src/PoolManager.sol:120). protocolFeesAccrued, balanceOf, isOperator and sync revert with no data for an address word with a high bit set, and balanceOf accepts an id with bit 200 set and returns 0, because the id is a full uint256 there (src/ERC6909.sol:17). The 24,050 byte Unichain contract gave the same answers as the other 20. Nine endpoints report the empty revert with data 0x and twelve with no data field, which is a property of the RPC server. These are answers of the deployed contracts. For the build under review the same rejection comes from the ABI decoder that solc 0.8.26 generates, which I could not run here.",
  RPC21+"; "+W+"width_probe.py and width_probe.out.json; src/types/PoolId.sol:11-15; src/PoolManager.sol:117-128, 279-297, 322-336")

C("Hook flag rule, fee flag bits, tick spacing bounds and price bounds as each network executes them, against the rule in the source text.",
  "All 21 deployed PoolManagers gave the answers the source predicts, word for word. A hook address with only bit 3, bit 2, bit 1 or bit 0 set is refused with HookAddressNotValid, and the pairs 0x0088, 0x0044, 0x0402 and 0x0101 and the pattern 0x0fff are accepted (src/libraries/Hooks.sol:109-120). An address with no flag is refused with a static fee and accepted with fee 0x800000, an address whose only set low bit is bit 14 is refused, and the zero address with fee 0x800000 is refused (Hooks.sol:124-126). A codeless hook with bit 13 or bit 12 makes initialize fail with InvalidHookResponse (Hooks.sol:152-154). Fee 1,000,000 is accepted, and 1,000,001, 0x400000, 0xC00000 and 0x800001 return LPFeeTooLarge with the value (src/libraries/LPFeeLibrary.sol:30-55). Spacing 0 and 32768 are refused and 32767 is accepted (src/PoolManager.sol:119-120). Equal and misordered currencies are refused (PoolManager.sol:121-125). MIN_SQRT_PRICE gives tick -887272, one below it and MAX_SQRT_PRICE give InvalidSqrtPrice, and MAX_SQRT_PRICE - 1 gives tick 887271 (src/libraries/TickMath.sol:127-129). In the source I enumerated all 16,384 flag patterns again and got 5,183 valid ones for a static fee, the number cycle 2 recorded.",
  RPC21+"; "+W+"width_probe.out.json; "+W+"packing.py; src/libraries/Hooks.sol:109-127")

C("Address words that reach CALL without a mask. Three assembly lines pass an address to CALL as it stands: the recipient of a native payout (src/types/Currency.sol:48), the token of an ERC-20 payout (Currency.sol:73) and the hook (src/libraries/Hooks.sol:134). Earlier readings listed the masked uses only. Can a word with bits above 160 make the PoolManager call a different account than the one it books, on a network whose CALL treats such a word differently?",
  "No. The three values come from ABI decoded arguments, which the first item shows are rejected when dirty, or from fromId, which truncates to 160 bits (src/types/Currency.sol:116-118). The debit is booked under the same value masked to 160 bits (src/libraries/CurrencyDelta.sol:12-13), so even a dirty word would name one account in both places as long as CALL uses the low 160 bits. I tested that on every network with one eth_call that has no to address and runs CALL, STATICCALL, EXTCODESIZE and BALANCE on a word with bits 160 and 200 to 231 set above the identity precompile address 0x04. On all 21 networks CALL and STATICCALL returned the 32 input bytes, which only 0x04 does, and BALANCE of the dirty zero address equalled BALANCE of address zero.",
  "rpc eth_call without a to address on the 21 rpc URLs of research/network-risk/state/networks.json, latest block on 2026-11-23; "+W+"calladdr_probe.py and calladdr_probe.out.json; src/types/Currency.sol:44-80; src/libraries/Hooks.sol:131-135")

C("Slot index folding on every network. F-040 showed that Avalanche treats slot S and S with bit 248 flipped as one storage slot and compared only Polygon and BNB Smart Chain. Does any of the 21 networks fold another single bit of a storage slot index or of a transient slot index, which could let two PoolManager slots (the four transient constants, a delta slot, a pool, tick or position slot) share one cell?",
  "Only Avalanche folds, only bit 248, and only for persistent storage. Test one: extsload(bytes32[]) on each deployed PoolManager for the 256 indexes that have exactly one bit set. Every word was zero except index 2 (the fee controller, where one is set) and, on Avalanche, index 2^248, which returned the owner stored in slot 0. Test two: one eth_call without a to address that stores 0xAA at storage slot 0 and 0xBB at transient slot 0 and then loads all 256 one-bit indexes of both kinds. Storage returned nonzero at bit 248 on Avalanche and nowhere else. Transient storage returned zero for all 256 indexes on all 21 networks. For the PoolManager on Avalanche the fold stays harmless: fields of one struct sit at S, S+1, S+2 and so on up to S+6 (src/libraries/Pool.sol:83-91, 68-77; src/libraries/Position.sol:19-25), and S xor (S+n) for n from 1 to 6 always has a set bit below bit 3, so it never equals 2^248. All other slots are keccak256 outputs, and the transient slots that carry the lock, the deltas and the synced currency are not folded anywhere. The probe sees a rule that drops one bit. It does not see a rule that needs two or more bits to change.",
  "rpc eth_call extsload(bytes32[]) on the 21 PoolManagers and eth_call without a to address on the 21 rpc URLs of research/network-risk/state/networks.json, latest block on 2026-11-23; "+W+"keyfold_probe.py and keyfold_probe.out.json; src/libraries/Lock.sol:8; src/libraries/CurrencyReserves.sol:11-13; src/libraries/NonzeroDeltaCount.sol:9-10")

C("Address folding for tokens that the node serves from a precompile. A currency id is the 160 bit address (src/types/Currency.sol:110-112). If a token dispatcher ignored some address bits, one token balance would answer under two currency ids, the situation of assumption A3. Do the one-bit neighbours of the precompile tokens the PoolManager holds answer balanceOf(PoolManager) like the token?",
  "No neighbour does. For three TIP-20 tokens on Tempo (0x20c0000000000000000000000000000000000000, 0x20c000000000000000000000030a4c7f7b8f3586, 0x20c0000000000000000000006a37da5c996874be), the USDC face 0x3600000000000000000000000000000000000000 on Arc and two B20 tokens on Base (0xB2000000000000000000002fEb517dFeC7415344, 0xB2000000000000000000000d8Ce462E99ee7A47B) I called balanceOf(PoolManager) at each of the 160 addresses that differ from the token in one bit. On Tempo 64 neighbours revert and 96 return empty data, on Base 73 revert and 87 return empty data, on Arc the three lowest bits revert and 157 return empty data. A revert or empty data makes sync fail at the decode of src/types/Currency.sol:94, so none of these addresses can carry a credit. The token itself returned a nonzero balance in all six cases. This covers aliases at distance one bit for six tokens and says nothing about other tokens or other distances.",
  "rpc eth_call balanceOf(address) on https://rpc.mainnet.tempo.xyz, https://rpc.mainnet.arc.io and https://mainnet.base.org/, latest block on 2026-11-23; "+W+"addrfold_probe.py and addrfold_probe.out.json; src/types/Currency.sol:90-96, 110-118",
  chains=[4217,5042,8453], lens='L4')

C("Storage addresses that coincide at run time for caller chosen input. The Solidity bug SOL-2026-7 (leads L-028, L-151, L-152) can only bite where two storage or memory addresses meet at run time. Which statements of src let a caller make two separately computed storage slots equal, and does the deployed code handle that case as the source says?",
  "One place: ERC6909.transfer and transferFrom with receiver equal to sender, where balanceOf[sender][id] is lowered and balanceOf[receiver][id] is raised through two slot computations (src/ERC6909.sol:25-48). Every other pair is kept apart by a check or lives in transient storage, which the affected optimizer steps do not track according to the Solidity post: currency0 is below currency1 (src/PoolManager.sol:121), tickLower is below tickUpper (src/libraries/Pool.sol:95), struct fields use fixed offsets from a pointer that is assigned once, and the hook and caller deltas are transient (src/libraries/CurrencyDelta.sol:28-41). I tested the one place with eth_simulateV1, which sends nothing: for a current holder of a claim, balanceOf, transfer of the whole balance to itself, balanceOf, transferFrom to itself, balanceOf, and a transfer of one unit more than the balance. On Ethereum, OP Mainnet, Unichain (the 24,050 byte contract), Ink, Arbitrum One, Robinhood Chain and Elysium the balance was the same before and after both self transfers and the oversized transfer reverted with an arithmetic panic. A stale read of the receiver balance would have doubled it. The endpoints of Avalanche, Monad, MegaETH, Arc and HyperEVM do not offer eth_simulateV1 and those of World Chain, Celo and X Layer refused it. On Base, Soneium, Polygon and BNB Smart Chain the log query for a holder was refused, and on Zora and Tempo I found no holder in the log window. Those 14 networks were not tested. This is a test of the deployed code on one path. It does not answer the three leads, which need a compiler.",
  "rpc eth_simulateV1 on the rpc URLs of chain ids 1, 10, 130, 57073, 42161, 4663 and 99901 at blocks 26480452, 159934823, 64720089, 61970076, 526923628, 121717107 and 10054728; "+W+"selfxfer.py and selfxfer_all.out.json; https://blog.soliditylang.org/2026/10/22/yul-optimizer-knowledge-base-stale-bookkeeping-bug/; src/ERC6909.sol:25-48",
  lens='L5')

C("The three storage readers and the transient reader, whose loops are the only handwritten assembly with an address variable that is reassigned (src/Extsload.sol:18-63, src/Exttload.sol:18-39): do the deployed loops return what a single read returns?",
  "Yes on all 21 networks. extsload(bytes32) for slots 0 to 6 equals eth_getStorageAt for the same slots. extsload(bytes32,uint256) from slot 0 with length 7 and extsload(bytes32[]) with the seven indexes both return the ABI array of those seven words. A length of 0 returns an empty array. A range that starts at index 2^256 - 1 with length 2 returns that slot and then slot 0, so the index wraps as the unchecked add at Extsload.sol:34 says. exttload of the lock slot, alone and in a list, returns zero outside a transaction. The readers only read, so their result matters for integrators that use StateLibrary and for no balance in the PoolManager.",
  "rpc eth_call and eth_getStorageAt on the 21 rpc URLs of research/network-risk/state/networks.json, latest block on 2026-11-23; "+W+"extsload_probe.py and extsload_probe.out.json; src/Extsload.sol:10-63; src/Exttload.sol:10-39")

C("Solidity bug list for the pinned compiler, fetched again on 2026-11-23 because the list changes after a release: is there an entry for 0.8.26 that cycle 2 did not see?",
  "No. bugs_by_version.json still lists the same eight entries for 0.8.26, and the newest version in the file is 0.8.39. Seven entries need constructs that src does not contain, as cycle 2 recorded. SOL-2026-7 (YulOptimizerKnowledgeBaseStaleBookkeeping, introduced in 0.8.19, fixed in 0.8.38, severity low) applies to any optimized via-IR build, which foundry.toml requests (solc 0.8.26, via_ir, optimizer_runs 44444444). The Solidity post says the condition cannot be decided from Solidity source. I had no compiler and installed none, so leads L-028, L-151 and L-152 stay open.",
  "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ethereum/solidity/develop/docs/bugs_by_version.json; https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ethereum/solidity/develop/docs/bugs.json; https://blog.soliditylang.org/2026/10/22/yul-optimizer-knowledge-base-stale-bookkeeping-bug/; foundry.toml:1-10")

C("Independent recount of every constant, mask, shift and cast in the non-test sources for this cycle, done from the text with new scripts and without the earlier transcriptions: Slot0, BalanceDelta, BeforeSwapDelta, the tick info slot, LiquidityMath, the fee fields, the hook flags, TickBitmap, the position hash, the delta slot hash, the transient slot constants, the error selectors, TickMath and BitMath.",
  "Everything matches its stated derivation, and I found no dirty bit, overlap or wrong width that an ordinary account can use. The four transient constants equal keccak256 of Unlocked, ReservesOf, Currency and NonzeroDeltaCount minus one, each written with 64 hex digits. The seven selectors written as literals match their signatures and 0x0f632fb3 is the xor of the seven ERC-6909 selectors. Every all-f literal has 6, 8, 16, 32 or 40 digits. isValidProtocolFee equals the plain rule for all 2^24 inputs, where cycle 2 tested one seventh of them. For each spacing from 1 to 32767 the assembly of tickSpacingToMaxLiquidityPerTick equals the reference formula with a dirty spacing word. The Slot0 setters and getters, the delta pack and unpack, the tick slot pack, addDelta, getSqrtPriceTarget and absDiff agree with exact integer arithmetic on 100,000 to 200,000 random cases each with random bits above the declared width. flipTick and nextInitializedTickWithinOneWord agree with a sorted list of ticks for eleven spacings including 1 and 32767, and the largest unchecked int24 product is 8,388,352, below 2^23. The 20 TickMath multipliers are within 0.5 of 2^128 / sqrt(1.0001)^(2^i), the price is strictly increasing over all 1,774,545 ticks, and getTickAtSqrtPrice inverts it on 180,000 boundary cases. Both BitMath functions agree with bit_length on all one-bit words and 300,000 random words. The unchecked blocks (30) and the SafeCast bounds read as the earlier cycles recorded.",
  W+"consts.py, tickmath.py, bitmath.py, packing.py; src/types/Slot0.sol:33-93; src/types/BalanceDelta.sol:14-71; src/types/BeforeSwapDelta.sol:9-37; src/libraries/Pool.sol:544-581; src/libraries/ProtocolFeeLibrary.sol:11-45; src/libraries/TickBitmap.sol:16-121; src/libraries/TickMath.sol:57-237; src/libraries/BitMath.sol:12-48; src/libraries/Position.sol:54-66; src/libraries/CurrencyDelta.sol:10-41; src/libraries/Lock.sol:8; src/libraries/CurrencyReserves.sol:11-13; src/libraries/NonzeroDeltaCount.sol:9-10")

leads=[
 {"chainIds":[143,4326,4217,5042,999,43114],"lens":"L2",
  "question":"Does the state lookup of the Monad, MegaETH, Tempo, Arc or HyperEVM client change a storage slot index, a transient slot index or an account address before it reads state, by a rule that maps two inputs to one entry and that needs more than one changed bit (a mask of several bits, a prefix rule, a bucket or page number used as the stored index)? On Avalanche, does the fold of bit 248 also apply to the slot indexes that eth_getProof and state sync report?",
  "why":"The one-bit probe of this cycle found the Avalanche fold of bit 248 and nothing else, and it cannot see a rule that needs two or more bits. The PoolManager keeps every pool, position and claim in slots derived by keccak256 (src/libraries/Pool.sol:83-91, src/ERC6909.sol:15-19) and the lock, deltas and synced currency in four constants and hashed transient slots (src/libraries/Lock.sol:8, src/libraries/CurrencyDelta.sol:10-16). A lossy rule in a client would let one account's slot stand in for another's. Client source is the only way to rule it out.",
  "priority":"low"},
 {"chainIds":[8453,7777777,480,1868,42220,196,137,56,43114,143,4326,4217,5042,999],"lens":"L5",
  "question":"On the 14 networks where eth_simulateV1 was refused, missing, or no claim holder was found in the log window, does a transfer or transferFrom of an ERC-6909 claim from a holder to itself leave balanceOf unchanged on the deployed PoolManager? Use debug_traceCall with a state diff where the endpoint allows it, or the forwarder stub of cycle 6.",
  "why":"Sender equal to receiver is the one input for which two separately computed storage slots of src coincide at run time (src/ERC6909.sol:25-48), which is the shape the Solidity bug SOL-2026-7 needs. The seven tested networks include both bytecode variants and behave as the source says, so a different answer is unlikely. The check closes the remaining endpoints for leads L-028 and L-151 without a compiler.",
  "priority":"low"}
]
out={"unit":"core-code-invariants-bits","profiles":[],"findings":[],"cleared":cleared,"leads":leads,"leadResults":[]}
